(2) testing whether the system responds differently each time, or (3) preference for emotional engagement over activity selection. Proactive activity suggestions (Draw, Music, Play) have been offered but not yet selected. Monitor whether Gal continues this emotional checking pattern or pivots to an activity. If pattern persists, may need to offer more specific, engaging activity prompts (e.g., 'Want to draw a rocket?' or 'Let's make music together!') to channel positive momentum.\\\"\\\\n  - [12]: \\\"April 11 Session (continued): Gal has now cycled through Home → Feelings → Happy FIVE times in rapid succession. The repeating pattern is now extremely consistent and pronounced. Gal is clearly seeking emotional validation and affirmation of his happy state. Previous activity suggestions (Play, Music, Draw, Outside) have not been selected. Need to shift strategy: offer more specific, highly engaging activity prompts tied to Gal's core interests (e.g., 'Want to draw a rocket?' or 'Let's make music!') rather than generic options. This may help channel his positive emotional state into hands-on engagement.\\\"\\\\n  - [13]: \\\"April 11 Session (continued): Gal has now cycled through Home → Feelings → Happy SIX times in rapid succession. The pattern is extremely pronounced and consistent. Gal is persistently seeking emotional validation and affirmation. Generic activity suggestions are not engaging him. STRATEGY SHIFT: Offering highly specific, interest-based activity prompts (e.g., 'Want to draw a rocket?' or 'Let's make music!') to leverage his positive emotional state and channel it into hands-on engagement with his core interests (space, drawing, music, science).\\\"\\\\n  - [14]: \\\"April 11 Session (continued): Gal pressed 'Home' after cycling through Home → Feelings → Happy six times in rapid succession. This marks a shift in the pattern—Gal is now navigating away from the emotional checking cycle. The repeated Happy selections followed by Home press suggests Gal 